course: dictionary
lesson: 4976
title: Incredibly: very, or hard to believe
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Incredibly is an adverb. It tells you how much another word is true, like turning up the volume. «It was incredibly cold» means it was extremely cold, much more than just cold.
explain: Look at the word it is joined to. In «incredibly lucky», the word lucky is made stronger. In «incredibly fast», the word fast is made stronger. Incredibly does not change the word it joins; it only makes it bigger.
explain: Sometimes incredibly comes at the start of a sentence, with a comma after it, as in «Incredibly, the cat survived the fall.» Now it means something is so surprising that it is hard to believe. Check the context: a cat falling and surviving is surprising, so that is your clue.
explain: Try a swap. Put very in place of incredibly. In «incredibly cold», very cold still makes sense, so the word means extremely. In «Incredibly, the cat survived», the swap does not fit well, so this is the second meaning.
fact: Incredibly means extremely or very, as in «incredibly fast».
fact: Incredibly can also mean in a way that is hard to believe, as in «Incredibly, the cat survived the fall».
fact: «Kateryna Bilokur, a self-taught Ukrainian painter, painted incredibly detailed flowers.» Here incredibly makes detailed stronger: her flowers were extremely detailed.
fact: «A computer is not clever the way you are; it is incredibly fast and incredibly obedient.» One sentence can use incredibly twice, with two describing words.
fact: A comma after incredibly at the start of a sentence is often a clue that it means hard to believe.
exercise: «It was incredibly cold, so we wore two coats.» What does incredibly mean here? | Quickly | *Extremely or very | Only sometimes | Slightly
exercise: «Incredibly, the cat survived the fall.» What does incredibly show? | Something that is always true | Something very big | *Something surprising and hard to believe | Something that happened slowly
exercise: Find the word that incredibly makes stronger in «an incredibly lucky boy». | *lucky | an | incredibly | boy
test: Which sentence uses incredibly to mean hard to believe? | *«Incredibly, the bird flew through the storm.» | «She was incredibly tall.» | «It was incredibly hot.»
test: Why is «incredibly fast» an example of an adverb? | It shows who is speaking | It is the name of a thing | It is a doing word | *It tells how much fast is true, making it stronger
exam: Which of these means the same as «incredibly detailed»? | Hidden from view | *Extremely detailed | Quickly drawn | Badly drawn
